# Naming Rule: Subject_Action

Enforce `Subject_Action` scheme on created or modified identifiers.

## Required Pattern & Regex

- Format: `Subject_Action` or `Subject_Action_Target`
- Separator: Underscore (`_`)
- Segment casing: PascalCase
- Validation Regex: `^[A-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]*_[A-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]*(_[A-Z][a-zA-Z0-9]*)?$`
- Primary keys / IDs: `Subject_ID`

## Target Scope

Apply naming scheme to:
- Variable names
- Function names
- Utility identifiers
- **Type / interface / object property keys** (including nested fields)
- URL query keys (`?Deal_Add=1`)
- Route parameters
- Form field `name` attributes (non-autofill)
- Storage keys (`localStorage`, `sessionStorage`)

Property keys are identifiers too. Do not leave camelCase fields under a `Subject_Action` type.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — type renamed, fields still camelCase
type Portal_Project_Money = {
  dealId: string;
  dealValue: number;
  paid: number;
  owed: number;
};

// ✅ GOOD
type Portal_Project_Money = {
  Deal_ID: string;
  Deal_Value: number;
  Money_Paid: number;
  Money_Owed: number;
};
```

## API / wire-format boundary

External JSON (DB rows, third-party APIs, browser `FormData` autofill) may stay camelCase or snake_case **at the edge only**. Map to `Subject_Action` in app types and UI code:

```typescript
const Money_Row: Portal_Project_Money = {
  Deal_ID: String(Raw_Value.deal_id),
  Deal_Value: Number(Raw_Value.deal_value),
  Money_Paid: Number(Raw_Value.paid),
  Money_Owed: Number(Raw_Value.owed),
};
```

Do not rename public HTTP response keys without an explicit migrate — prefer mapping in the route/loader instead.

## Anti-Patterns (Banned)

- camelCase: `usersCreate`, `addDeal`, `dealId`
- snake_case: `users_create`, `add_deal`, `deal_id` (except raw DB/API edge)
- PascalCase without underscore: `UsersCreate`, `AddDeal`, `DealId`
- Vague actions: `Users_DoThing`, `Data_Process`
- One-word properties: `paid`, `owed`, `name` → use `Money_Paid`, `Money_Owed`, `Contact_Name` (or clear Subject_Action pair)

## Code Examples

INVALID:
```typescript
const addDeal = (orderid: string) => {
  const usersData = fetchUser();
  window.location.href = `/deals?addDeal=1&orderId=${orderid}`;
};
```

VALID:
```typescript
const Deal_Add = (Order_ID: string) => {
  const Users_Data = User_Fetch();
  window.location.href = `/deals?Deal_Add=1&Order_ID=${Order_ID}`;
};
```

## Refactoring Scope Rules

- Edit active scope only. Rename noncompliant symbols inside touched block — **including object/type properties in that block**.
- Preserve program behavior completely (update all reads/writes of renamed keys in scope).
- Leave untouched code alone. Do not refactor whole file.
- Ask user before renaming exported or public API symbols / wire JSON keys.
